Some real-world actions need **more than one** change to happen together, or not at all. Transferring money between two accounts needs to subtract from one and add to the other — if only one of those succeeds, the data is now wrong. A **transaction** groups multiple statements into a single all-or-nothing unit:

```sql
BEGIN;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE account_id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE account_id = 2;

COMMIT;
```

`BEGIN` starts the transaction, and `COMMIT` makes both changes permanent together. If anything goes wrong before COMMIT, you run `ROLLBACK` instead — undoing everything since BEGIN, as if none of it happened.

```sql
BEGIN;

UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE account_id = 1;
-- something looks wrong here — maybe account 2 doesn't actually exist

ROLLBACK;
```

Without transactions, that first UPDATE would already be permanent, leaving account 1 short $100 with nothing added anywhere. ROLLBACK is the safety net that undoes everything since BEGIN, so a problem partway through never leaves the data in a half-finished, inconsistent state.
